# Spec-driven development

Docs in `docs/` are the source of truth. Code implements an accepted requirement and does not invent product behavior.

## Before changing the product

1. Read `docs/README.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, `docs/open-questions.md`, and the requirement you are implementing.
2. If no accepted requirement covers the work, stop. Draft one with the `req` skill. Do not code ahead of acceptance. `/build` implements a single accepted requirement.
3. If the code and a doc disagree, follow the doc. Ask the user before changing what or why.
4. If a fact is listed in `docs/open-questions.md`, ask. Do not pick a gateway, language, font, or market to unblock yourself.

## Traceability

- A change maps to one requirement id, such as `REQ-003`.
- Jest tests for that work include the same id in the name or description.
- A new backend, CMS, admin, or payment integration needs an ADR first. ADRs are few and only for hard-to-reverse choices.

## Example

User: "Add a newsletter form."

Wrong: build the form and choose a provider.

Right: check `docs/open-questions.md` and existing requirements. Draft or update the requirement. Wait until it is `accepted`. Then implement only that scope.
